Result: 110 passed, 0 failed, 0 errors across all four modules
(purekafka 13, purerestlib 45, inventoryService 23, orderService 29).
scalafmtCheck: clean, no reformatting needed.

What this proves: the new modules/purekafka sbt module exists, resolves
its dependencies (cats-effect, fs2-kafka, cats-retry,
resilience4j-circuitbreaker, otel4s-oteljava(+testkit), log4cats), and is
aggregated into the root build. Three generic, non-HTTP-coupled
combinators are implemented and tested:

- Retry.middleware[F, A]: retries any raised error (by default), up to
  RetryConfig.maxRetries with exponential backoff, recording a
  purekafka.retry.attempts metric with the same retried/succeeded/
  exhausted shape purerest.resilience.Retry uses. Hand-rolled via
  cats-retry's retryingOnErrors (not delegated to http4s's Retry client
  middleware, which doesn't apply to a non-HTTP call) - the same pattern
  gluon's OrderEventPublisher/PaymentEventPublisher already use directly.
  Gained an optional nonRetriable: Throwable => Boolean hook (default
  _ => false) during this phase, needed for correct composition with
  CircuitBreaker (see below).

- CircuitBreaker.middleware[F, A]: wraps resilience4j-circuitbreaker's
  core engine, same CircuitBreakerConfig/CircuitBreakerOpen shape as the
  HTTP version, simplified to use resilience4j's onSuccess/onError hooks
  directly (no isFailureResult value-inspecting predicate needed, since a
  Kafka-style F[A] call has no "successful-looking value that's actually
  a failure" case the way an HTTP 5xx response does). Three metrics:
  purekafka.circuit_breaker.state_transitions / calls_rejected / state.

- Resilience.middleware[F, A]: composes Retry (outer) around
  CircuitBreaker (inner), mirroring purerest.resilience.Resilience's
  documented ordering exactly, including that a breaker-open rejection is
  not itself retried (via Retry's new nonRetriable hook, set to
  _ == CircuitBreakerOpen).

Design deviation from a literal reading of ADR 0010's "F[A] => F[A]"
phrasing, made for correctness: introduced a shared purekafka.resilience.Guard[F]
trait (construct once per config/meter, apply[A] many times) rather than
a plain function value. Necessary because resilience4j's CircuitBreaker
engine is genuinely stateful (failure counts, open/closed/half-open) and
must be the SAME instance across every call it guards - a fresh breaker
constructed per call would never accumulate failures, making the whole
breaker a no-op. Guard mirrors how Client[F] is built once and .run()
called many times in purerest.resilience's own HTTP version; Retry and
Resilience also return Guard[F] for API consistency and so they compose
cleanly.

Two bugs found and fixed via TDD before this checkpoint, not left for a
later phase:
1. Retry originally retried every error unconditionally, which would
   have retried a CircuitBreakerOpen rejection too when composed -
   directly contradicting this track's own plan.md. Fixed by adding the
   nonRetriable hook described above.
2. ResilienceSuite's first draft of its "breaker opens after threshold"
   test assumed each top-level guard(fa) call maps to exactly one
   circuit-breaker failure event. Actually false: Retry retries by
   re-running the entire breaker-wrapped computation, not just the inner
   fa, so each individual retry attempt hits the breaker separately too.
   Rewrote that test with maxRetries=0 to isolate the scenario cleanly,
   and rewrote the "not retried" test to assert on the breaker's own
   calls_rejected metric (1 rejection, not 2) rather than on the
   underlying fa-call count, which can't distinguish "retried and
   rejected again" from "failed fast" (fa never runs on a rejected
   attempt either way).

Not yet built (Phases 2-5, per plan.md): consumer stream supervision,
dead-letter routing, cross-cutting observability (tracing/consumer-lag/
structured logging), and the load test. This phase is resilience only.
